    "name":  "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis",
    "module":  "respiratory",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), also known as extrinsic allergic alveolitis, is an immune-mediated inflammatory lung disease caused by repeated inhalation of antigenic organic particles. Common antigens include thermophilic actinomycetes in mouldy hay (farmer\u0027s lung), bird proteins (bird fancier\u0027s lung), and fungal antigens in hot tubs or humidifiers. Acute HP presents with flu-like symptoms and breathlessness 4–8 hours after exposure. Chronic HP develops with ongoing exposure and resembles usual interstitial pneumonia on HRCT, with progressive breathlessness and fibrosis. Diagnosis requires the clinical triad of exposure, radiology, and either BAL lymphocytosis or specific IgG antibodies. Avoidance of the antigen is the primary treatment.",
